WEBVTT

00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:07.716
Welcome to CSC4801, AI-assisted Software Engineering.

00:00:07.716 --> 00:00:11.260
This is Lecture Three: Advanced Agents.

00:00:12.033 --> 00:00:15.209
Last time, we gave a language model a body.

00:00:15.209 --> 00:00:21.193
With a harness around it, the model can look at files, act on them, and run code.

00:00:21.543 --> 00:00:23.471
Here is the loop we built.

00:00:23.471 --> 00:00:33.183
Prepare the context, call the LLM, run the tools it asks for, add the results, and go around again, until no more tools are needed.

00:00:33.533 --> 00:00:36.983
This loop works, but it has several problems.

00:00:36.983 --> 00:00:39.053
Today we fix three of them.

00:00:39.403 --> 00:00:43.925
First, context management: what goes into each call.

00:00:43.925 --> 00:00:51.578
Second, retrieval-augmented generation: how an agent remembers things for the long term.

00:00:51.578 --> 00:00:56.883
Third, subagents: how to split one job across several agents.

00:00:57.483 --> 00:01:01.203
For each fix, watch where it plugs into the loop.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.341
Let's start with context.

00:01:04.341 --> 00:01:09.960
An LLM is stateless. It remembers nothing between two calls.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:16.879
So every time the agent calls the model, it sends all the historical messages again:

00:01:16.879 --> 00:01:21.920
the system prompt, the request, every reply, and every tool result.

00:01:22.270 --> 00:01:25.672
That makes the loop very hungry for tokens.

00:01:25.672 --> 00:01:33.190
Read 10 files, run the tests 3 times, and edit 5 times, and you can easily pass 100,000 tokens.

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:37.279
And each new call carries everything that came before it.

00:01:37.279 --> 00:01:40.100
The context keeps growing, turn after turn.

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:41.509
But wait.

00:01:41.509 --> 00:01:45.980
Context windows keep getting bigger. Why not just send everything?

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:49.393
It's true that windows have grown fast.

00:01:49.393 --> 00:01:59.160
Back in 2024, Google's Gemini 1.5 could almost fit the whole Harry Potter and Lord of the Rings series in its 2 million token window.

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:01.730
Today, some models offer even more.

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:04.365
But a long context may fail.

00:02:04.365 --> 00:02:12.120
A benchmark called RULER found that almost all models lose accuracy as the context gets longer.

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:16.143
Another study is called Lost in the Conversation.

00:02:16.143 --> 00:02:25.435
When a task arrives piece by piece over many turns, models jump to early answers, make wrong assumptions, and then get stuck on them.

00:02:25.435 --> 00:02:28.160
Their results become far less reliable.

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:30.646
So the lesson is clear.

00:02:30.646 --> 00:02:34.270
Historical messages need to be concise.

00:02:34.870 --> 00:02:38.442
In fact, a lot loads before you even type anything:

00:02:38.442 --> 00:02:43.906
the system prompt, your CLAUDE.md file, memory, skills, and tool descriptions.

00:02:43.906 --> 00:02:48.390
Claude Code has a page that shows exactly what fills its window.

00:02:49.167 --> 00:02:50.823
But there is a trade-off.

00:02:50.823 --> 00:02:55.195
Shorter context is good, but we also want to use the prompt cache.

00:02:55.195 --> 00:02:57.447
These two pull against each other.

00:02:57.797 --> 00:03:00.084
Here's how the prompt cache works.

00:03:00.084 --> 00:03:03.718
Call one sends the user's message: add error handling.

00:03:03.718 --> 00:03:09.437
Call two sends that same message, plus the assistant's reply about missing arguments.

00:03:09.787 --> 00:03:14.959
The server notices that call two starts with exactly the same text as call one.

00:03:14.959 --> 00:03:20.067
This is prefix matching, and it lets the server reuse the work it already did.

00:03:20.417 --> 00:03:27.457
Call three adds one more message, which asks to change the code, and it reuses the shared prefix again.

00:03:27.807 --> 00:03:28.508
Why care?

00:03:28.508 --> 00:03:31.158
Look at one provider's price list.

00:03:31.158 --> 00:03:37.550
Input tokens that hit the cache cost about 50 times less than tokens that miss it.

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:39.967
And they come back faster, too.

00:03:40.567 --> 00:03:44.705
This is also why the system prompt goes before the user prompt.

00:03:44.705 --> 00:03:52.127
The system prompt is usually fixed, so putting it first keeps the prefix the same, and the cache hits more often.

00:03:52.477 --> 00:03:54.806
So here is the design question.

00:03:54.806 --> 00:04:00.517
How do we cut context length, while causing as few cache misses as possible?

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:06.126
The Claude Code agent uses five strategies, in two groups.

00:04:06.126 --> 00:04:10.620
The first three cut things out. The last two compress.

00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:14.873
Strategy one is a budget on the size of each tool result.

00:04:14.873 --> 00:04:19.050
It works at step seven, where the results of tools come back.

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:22.474
Some results are huge, like a long log file.

00:04:22.474 --> 00:04:29.040
If a result is longer than a set threshold, the agent writes it into a temporary file instead.

00:04:29.390 --> 00:04:37.270
In the context, it keeps only a short preview, like: saved to a temp file, 80,000 characters in total.

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:38.677
Nothing is lost.

00:04:38.677 --> 00:04:43.500
If the model needs the details later, it can simply read that file again.

00:04:44.267 --> 00:04:46.977
Strategy two is a tool count budget.

00:04:46.977 --> 00:04:52.547
Instead of the size of one result, it limits how many old results we keep.

00:04:52.897 --> 00:04:59.572
Tools that only look, such as Read, Bash, Grep, and Glob, keep just the 5 most recent results.

00:04:59.572 --> 00:05:04.897
Older ones are replaced with a short note: old tool result content cleared.

00:05:05.247 --> 00:05:06.582
Why is that safe?

00:05:06.582 --> 00:05:09.172
Because a read can be done again.

00:05:09.172 --> 00:05:13.647
If the model needs that file, it just reads it once more.

00:05:14.247 --> 00:05:19.098
Tools that change things, Edit and Write, keep all their results.

00:05:19.098 --> 00:05:24.247
You cannot redo an edit to see what it was, so that record must stay.

00:05:25.034 --> 00:05:28.296
Strategy three is a conversation round budget.

00:05:28.296 --> 00:05:34.394
A round is one assistant message, plus all the tool calls and results that go with it.

00:05:34.744 --> 00:05:37.582
A long task can run for 70 rounds or more.

00:05:37.582 --> 00:05:43.664
And in the old rounds, the agent may have explored wrong directions that no longer matter.

00:05:44.014 --> 00:05:50.934
So the agent removes the oldest rounds, and keeps the recent ones that describe the current work.

00:05:51.701 --> 00:05:53.697
Now the compress group.

00:05:53.697 --> 00:05:56.301
Strategy four is auto compact.

00:05:56.651 --> 00:06:03.051
It is triggered when, even after cutting, the context still fills about 83% of the window.

00:06:03.401 --> 00:06:07.319
Then the agent calls the LLM to summarize the messages.

00:06:07.319 --> 00:06:15.441
The summary follows a checklist: the files recently modified, the agent's status, the plan, and the tools it used.

00:06:15.791 --> 00:06:22.551
Compression can happen in two places: on the agent side, the client, and on the model provider's server.

00:06:22.901 --> 00:06:26.719
Strategy five is the last resort: context collapse.

00:06:26.719 --> 00:06:36.901
If, after all of this, the window is still almost full, the agent saves everything to disk and restarts the session with a fresh window.

00:06:37.501 --> 00:06:39.475
None of these ideas are fancy.

00:06:39.475 --> 00:06:43.621
They are solid, practical engineering methods used in industry.

00:06:43.971 --> 00:06:45.682
Pause the video for a moment.

00:06:45.682 --> 00:06:52.171
If you were designing your own context manager, what would you cut first, and what would you never throw away?

00:06:52.968 --> 00:06:55.698
Everything so far is short-term memory.

00:06:55.698 --> 00:07:02.208
It's like a computer's RAM: it lives inside one session, and it's gone when the session ends.

00:07:02.558 --> 00:07:04.793
But what about long-term memory?

00:07:04.793 --> 00:07:10.798
How does the agent carry knowledge from one session to the next, and into the project?

00:07:11.348 --> 00:07:13.453
We already have a simple answer.

00:07:13.453 --> 00:07:16.348
The agent has tools to read and write files.

00:07:16.698 --> 00:07:26.316
A memory.md file holds things like a description of the user, their feedback and preferences, the project status, and reference documents.

00:07:26.316 --> 00:07:28.965
The agent writes it and reads it back.

00:07:28.965 --> 00:07:30.498
That's dynamic memory.

00:07:30.848 --> 00:07:36.018
A CLAUDE.md file is written by you, the user, and rarely changes.

00:07:36.018 --> 00:07:37.688
That's static memory.

00:07:38.468 --> 00:07:44.298
But let's look at a more advanced technique: retrieval-augmented generation, or RAG.

00:07:44.298 --> 00:07:46.588
It is built on a vector database.

00:07:46.938 --> 00:07:54.138
Each piece of information is turned into a vector of numbers, called an embedding, and stored in the database.

00:07:54.488 --> 00:07:57.776
For example, we store three coding rules.

00:07:57.776 --> 00:08:02.828
Boolean checking functions should start with the prefix `has_`.

00:08:02.828 --> 00:08:05.635
Each file has at most 30 functions.

00:08:05.635 --> 00:08:08.682
And folders are at most 5 layers deep.

00:08:08.682 --> 00:08:11.088
Each rule gets its own vector.

00:08:11.438 --> 00:08:15.049
Now the user asks: add a function to check status.

00:08:15.049 --> 00:08:21.550
That query is also turned into a vector, and the database finds the closest stored vector.

00:08:21.550 --> 00:08:26.678
Here, it's the rule about boolean functions, with a similarity of 0.96.

00:08:27.028 --> 00:08:31.757
The agent adds that rule to the prompt, and sends both to the LLM.

00:08:31.757 --> 00:08:36.988
Now the model knows to call its new function something like `has_status`.

00:08:37.588 --> 00:08:39.561
Real systems go further.

00:08:39.561 --> 00:08:44.988
A system called Mem0 also merges memories that conflict or repeat.

00:08:45.768 --> 00:08:50.008
RAG sounds great, but it has three challenges.

00:08:50.358 --> 00:08:53.236
First, similarity is not relevance.

00:08:53.236 --> 00:09:04.173
Ask, does this code have bugs, and the closest vectors might be about where bug reports are stored, or running tests before a commit.

00:09:04.173 --> 00:09:09.518
Only one of them, a common bug is unchecked inputs, really helps.

00:09:10.118 --> 00:09:12.388
Second, it's hard to maintain.

00:09:12.388 --> 00:09:17.004
There are many vendors, such as Pinecone, Qdrant, and Milvus.

00:09:17.004 --> 00:09:24.118
And you must choose the embedding setup and chunk size, update the index, and merge conflicts.

00:09:24.468 --> 00:09:26.162
Third, it's unreadable.

00:09:26.162 --> 00:09:29.330
A human can open a memory file and read it.

00:09:29.330 --> 00:09:31.908
Nobody can read a table of vectors.

00:09:32.258 --> 00:09:36.632
And memory isn't the only thing an agent must bring into a project.

00:09:36.632 --> 00:09:39.178
Security policies need to reach it too.

00:09:39.528 --> 00:09:45.121
So which is better, a simple memory file or a vector database?

00:09:45.121 --> 00:09:48.008
There is no absolute answer yet.

00:09:48.801 --> 00:09:51.843
So what is a good memory design?

00:09:51.843 --> 00:09:56.121
Research points to one idea: structure helps.

00:09:56.671 --> 00:10:06.671
One line of work stores an agent's past experience at different levels: the whole trajectory, a reusable workflow, or a short summary of the insight.

00:10:07.021 --> 00:10:09.270
Another treats the user as code.

00:10:09.270 --> 00:10:14.261
Start from a raw conversation between two people over several sessions.

00:10:14.611 --> 00:10:21.487
Phase one: an LLM pulls out every fact as a flat string, and only ever appends to the list.

00:10:21.487 --> 00:10:24.811
That gives more than a thousand loose facts.

00:10:25.161 --> 00:10:35.903
Phase two: an LLM rewrites those facts as typed Python objects, like a Person class with a list of jobs and a list of passions.

00:10:35.903 --> 00:10:41.401
Dates become real dates, and repeated facts merge into one place.

00:10:42.001 --> 00:10:46.881
Structured memory like this is easier to query, and easier to check.

00:10:47.668 --> 00:10:52.071
So far we have short-term and long-term memory for one agent.

00:10:52.071 --> 00:10:54.308
Now the third topic: subagents.

00:10:54.658 --> 00:10:56.056
Here's the problem.

00:10:56.056 --> 00:11:04.298
We ask one agent: add a function that calculates the discount price for premium users, and write code and tests.

00:11:04.648 --> 00:11:08.354
It writes a function that returns 80% of the price.

00:11:08.354 --> 00:11:12.422
Then it writes a test that only checks the premium case.

00:11:12.422 --> 00:11:16.128
The test passes, but the test cases are incomplete.

00:11:16.478 --> 00:11:16.849
Why?

00:11:16.849 --> 00:11:23.988
The test was written by the same agent, with the code already in its context.

00:11:23.988 --> 00:11:28.067
It tested what it wrote, not what was asked.

00:11:28.067 --> 00:11:30.478
This is context pollution.

00:11:31.078 --> 00:11:40.382
With subagents, the main agent gives one subagent the job of writing the code, and a different subagent the job of writing the tests.

00:11:40.382 --> 00:11:46.678
The test writer sees only the task, so it also checks a regular user, who pays full price.

00:11:47.028 --> 00:11:49.376
Subagents bring three advantages.

00:11:49.376 --> 00:11:55.068
First, context isolation: each one has its own context window and its own tools.

00:11:55.418 --> 00:12:01.738
Second, specialization: one can be a UX design expert, another a security expert.

00:12:02.088 --> 00:12:07.048
Third, parallelization: many subagents can work at the same time.

00:12:07.398 --> 00:12:18.314
In Claude Code, you define a subagent in a markdown file: a name, a description, the tools it may use, the model, and then its own system prompt.

00:12:18.314 --> 00:12:22.078
The main agent starts it just like calling a tool.

00:12:22.868 --> 00:12:26.068
Let's sum up, on the loop from last time.

00:12:26.418 --> 00:12:29.536
Context management works at steps 3 and 7.

00:12:29.536 --> 00:12:36.738
Cut old and oversized results, compress when the window fills, and keep the prompt cache hitting.

00:12:37.088 --> 00:12:42.008
RAG gives the agent long-term memory, retrieved while preparing the context.

00:12:42.358 --> 00:12:48.398
And subagents run as tools at step 6, each with a clean context of its own.

00:12:48.748 --> 00:12:50.958
Now the context is well managed.

00:12:50.958 --> 00:12:55.378
The next problem is that the agent's built-in tools are limited.

00:12:55.378 --> 00:12:56.828
That's for next time.

00:12:57.178 --> 00:12:58.939
Here's something to try.

00:12:58.939 --> 00:13:04.809
In Claude Code, type /context during a long task, and see what fills the window.

00:13:04.809 --> 00:13:08.258
Can you spot which strategy will kick in first?

00:13:09.035 --> 00:13:10.829
That's all for this lecture.

00:13:10.829 --> 00:13:15.315
Thank you for watching, and see you in the next class, MCP and Skills.

